---
import Head from "../components/head.astro";
import getFalafel from "../lib/falafelLoader";

const falafel = getFalafel();

const writeDateString = "4 Sep 2026";
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
    <Head title="About Duncan’s Wrap Map" />
    <body>
        <main class="text-container">
            <header>
                <nav>
                    <span>
                        <a href="/" title="back! to the home page"
                            >https://falafel.fyi</a
                        >
                        &nbsp;/&nbsp;
                        <h1>
                            <a
                                href={"/about"}
                                title={"about falafel.fyi (you're already here!)"}
                                >about</a
                            >
                        </h1>
                    </span>
                    <span title={writeDateString}
                        >{writeDateString.replaceAll("0", "o")}</span
                    >
                </nav>
            </header>
            <article>
                <p>
                    This is a list of all the places I’ve eaten falafel wraps.
                    There are <strong>{falafel.length}</strong> of them. Just one
                    more and then I’ll be happy, right?
                </p>
                <p>
                    I’ve eaten a lot of falafel in my time. I’ve done other
                    things, too. I’ve picked gooseberries in the summer sun,
                    climbed mountains in the driving rain, and seen whales
                    breach the surface of the desperate North Atlantic. I’ve
                    laughed, and cried, and loved, and lost. I’ve even made
                    websites. But if nothing else, I’ve eaten a <strong
                        >lot</strong
                    > of falafel wraps. And I’ve enjoyed almost every one.
                </p>
                <p>
                    I haven’t been to Egypt, or Palestine, or Lebanon, or Yemen,
                    or Israel. I probably haven’t had real falafel. Critics will
                    denounce me for a falafel map that’s mostly set in England.
                    And they’ll be right. I’m a faux-falafel philistine, a
                    Levantine lout, a Coptic cheat. I’m eating fake falafel, fib
                    falafel, fraud falafel. My falafel is pantomime falafel,
                    costume falafel. I’m watching shadows of falafel on the wall
                    of Plato’s cave, and I have the audacity to make a website
                    called <a href="/" title="https://falafel.fyi"
                        >https://falafel.fyi</a
                    >.
                </p>
                <h2>How to use this website</h2>
                <p>
                    I’ve had people (friends, even) tell me this map is useless.
                    What’s the point of falafel reviews if they’re all Five
                    Stars?
                </p>
                <p>
                    That’s the conceit of the map, of course. Every falafel wrap
                    is a good falafel wrap.
                </p>
                <p>
                    This isn’t a selective list. Some of the falafel here is
                    better, and some of it is worse. If I eat a falafel wrap, it
                    goes on the falafel map.
                </p>
                <p>
                    It’s not a checklist, or a best-of, or a watch mojo top 10.
                    It’s a map. If you’re somewhere on Earth and you’re feeling
                    peckish, check the map! There might be falafel nearby.
                </p>
                <p>
                    You can read the review! If it’s really bad, I probably say
                    that. If it’s really good, I probably say that too.
                </p>
                <p>
                    Best-of lists are a false promise, I think. A shortcut
                    without a view. You think you can just eat the best falafel
                    and be done with it? Here’s a horrible fact: most falafel
                    wraps are pretty good. If you stick to street vendors, food
                    trucks, and family restaurants, chances are you’ll like your
                    falafel. And what if you don’t? Who cares? There are joys in
                    every soggy crevice. You’ve tried, you’ve learned, you’ve
                    lived.
                </p>
                <p>
                    Imagine: it is the incandescent underbelly of the night. You
                    are hungry, or, rather, you could eat, and you find yourself
                    in the womblike embrace of a midnight falafel shop. The
                    people inside are arguing about stamp duty and football and
                    how on earth they'll get home now that the trains aren’t
                    running. You pay for a falafel wrap, off a menu where
                    falafel is clearly an afterthought. Everyone else is eating
                    lamb. It’s not nuanced falafel, and if it was noon you’d be
                    more critical. But it’s salty and fatty and acidic and hot,
                    and you could knock someone unconscious with the heft of it,
                    and it’s exactly what you’ve needed ever since you forgot to
                    eat dinner eight hours ago. Sounds like five stars to you.
                </p>
                <p>
                    Imagine: you’ve just run home from the falafel shop, wrap in
                    hand like a relay baton, and when you tear open the paper
                    you find that the force of your stride, the momentum of your
                    pumping arms, has upset the wrap’s delicate ecosystem, and
                    that the wrap is now dry, drained of tahini, that all the
                    sauce has filtered down through falafel and pooled at the
                    buckled-up bottom of the wrap. You smile at the fluid
                    dynamics of it, the gravity of the situation, the laws of
                    physics made real, and when you’re done eating your dry wrap
                    you drink the sauce like broth from folds of the soggy wax
                    paper, content in the knowledge that the meal will be whole
                    once more in your stomach.
                </p>
                <p>
                    Imagine you're sitting on the edge of the quay, and the sun
                    has almost set. You had lunch hours ago, and you meant to
                    have dinner, really, you did, but here you are, a few pints
                    deep in a city you don't know, and you've just left the
                    meet-and-greet. On the way back to the hostel you saw
                    Falafel King, or Count Falafel, or Falafel Czar, or
                    something like that, and it did not look good, you remember
                    thinking, but the smell was so warm and the sign was so
                    flashing and you were so hungry. So now here you are, at the
                    edge of the quay, encircled by gulls in a city you do not
                    know, and you're trying to peel the wrapping paper from the
                    pitta bread, and it's not going well. Despite your
                    scientific precision, your gymnastic understanding of the
                    eating of the wrap, you can't do anything to prevent the
                    bread from tearing. You knew it was too long on the grill.
                    The man wrapped up your wrap in wax paper and threw it on
                    the grill, and you knew it was too long when the card reader
                    stuttered, and the seconds ticked by, and you had to shuffle
                    through your pockets for cash, and the paper browned, fusing
                    inwards, sealing itself against the bread, sealing your
                    untoward fate. Sometimes you have the patience, or the desk
                    space, to carefully extract a too-grilled wrap from its
                    too-grilled paper wrapping, but now you have neither, and
                    the gulls are getting closer, and your hands slip again,
                    tear, and the hummus starts oozing, and you have to act
                    fast. There is no other way.
                </p>
                <p>
                    And then you are done. Falafel inhaled. You are picking bits
                    of wax paper from your gums, and you are licking tahini from
                    your lips, and despite yourself, you are smiling.
                </p>
                <p>
                    Imagine you’ve just walked fifteen miles, a much noisier
                    hike than you bargained for. The ramblers’ guide you found
                    online didn’t mention that the route followed the M25. The
                    pictures all faced outwards, to Hertfordshire, and not
                    inwards, to the exhaust and the dust and the screaming
                    lorries. There was fox-piss on the pavement, and cans of
                    nitrous in the weeds, and nettles waist-high leaning into
                    the trail. The blackberries you saw in the pictures, so
                    plump, so ripe, were actually shriveled, all covered in
                    soot. But now it is the end of the walk, and you have come
                    to a town, spilled out from that harrowing path and into a
                    public park where children are running in circles on the
                    grass and a four-piece band is playing oldies. And of course
                    they are - they’re oldies themselves. The singer is a
                    grandpa, the pianist is a grandma, the drummer is an uncle’s
                    uncle. You are so charmed, and the stress of the day, all
                    the cortisol welled up in the cracks of your mind, starts to
                    thin, and you exhale, and imagine yourself laying down in
                    the grass. But you are hungry. Fifteen miles hungry. The
                    blackberries you were promised were all covered in fumes,
                    and the nuts you brought were gone by midmorning, and you
                    must eat now, because if you don’t, you never will; you’ll
                    sink into the earth and the grass will cover you whole and
                    you’ll never get the train, then, you’ll never get home. And
                    falafel, says Google Maps, falafel is only a hundred meters
                    away. So you gather yourself. You scoop up your heart and
                    your weary, pumping lungs, and you drag yourself to *Burger
                    Inn Cafe Kebab & Restaurant*, which probably has pretty good
                    food.
                </p>
                <p>
                    It does not. The wrap is a dry, cold Tesco tortilla, filled
                    with shredded carrot, a few balls of microwave falafel,
                    sweet chili sauce, and more shredded carrot. Somehow, it is
                    mostly air.
                </p>
                <p>
                    But when you bite into it, you are in the grass, legs
                    splayed out like pick-up-sticks, swept up by the breeze and
                    the birdsong and the septuagenarian rock and roll, and you
                    hardly notice how bad it is.
                </p>
                <hr />
                <p>
                    When you’re pushing the boundaries of what it means to be
                    alive, how could anything <i>not</i> be five stars? There is beauty
                    everywhere, if only you have the eyes to see. If only you have
                    the tongue to taste. As Voltaire would say, this truly is the
                    best of all possible worlds.
                </p>
            </article>
        </main>
    </body>
</html>

<style>
    :root {
        /* font-size: clamp(1.3125rem, 1.1951rem + 0.5872vi, 1.75rem); */
    }

    body {
        display: grid;
        grid-template-columns: 1fr auto 1.618fr;
    }

    main {
        max-width: 45ch;
        margin-bottom: 50svh;
        grid-column: 2;
        margin-inline: 1rem;
        box-sizing: border-box;
    }

    header {
        min-height: 50svh;

        nav {
            position: sticky;
            top: 0;
            padding-top: 1rem;
            display: flex;
            justify-content: space-between;
            flex-wrap: wrap;

            @media screen and (max-width: 58ch) {
                flex-direction: column;
            }

            h1 {
                display: inline;
            }

            a:hover,
            a:focus-visible {
                text-decoration: underline;
                text-decoration-thickness: 0.0875em;
                text-underline-offset: 0.125em;
            }

            a:focus-visible,
            a:active {
                outline: none;
                background-color: var(--color-yellow);
            }
        }
    }

    article {
        h2 {
            font-size: 1.618rem;
            font-weight: 500;
            margin-top: 50svh;
        }

        hr {
            border: none;
            height: 30svh;
        }

        p {
            /* text-align: justify; */
            hyphens: auto;
            -ms-hyphens: auto;
            -moz-hyphens: auto;
            -webkit-hyphens: auto;

            line-height: 1.5;
            text-wrap: pretty;
        }

        :not(p) + p,
        p:first-of-type {
            text-indent: 4em;
        }

        p:last-of-type {
            &::after {
                content: "✴︎";
                margin-left: 0.5ch;
            }
        }

        strong {
            font-family: var(--kyroh);
            font-weight: normal;
            font-style: normal;
            line-height: 1;
            text-shadow: var(--yellow-shadow);
        }

        em {
            font-weight: bold;
            font-style: normal;
        }

        a:not(sup a) {
            text-decoration: underline;
            text-decoration-thickness: 2px;
        }

        a:not(sup a):hover,
        a:not(sup a):focus-visible {
            text-decoration: none;
            background-color: var(--color-black);
            color: var(--color-white);
            outline-color: var(--color-black);
            outline-width: 0.1em;
            outline-style: solid;
            outline-offset: 0;
            border-radius: 0.1em;
        }

        p:has(a:not(sup a):hover, a:not(sup a):focus-visible)::after {
            content: "→";
            font-family: var(--kyroh);
            font-size: 1.5em;
            display: inline-block;
            transform: translate(0.14em, 0.27em) rotate(-30deg);
            line-height: 0;
            width: 0;
            height: 0;
        }

        sup {
            line-height: 0.5;
            font-weight: 500;

            a:hover,
            a:focus-visible {
                background-color: var(--color-yellow);
                outline: none;
                text-decoration: underline;
            }
        }
    }
</style>
